	abstract = {Heterogeneous-agent consumption–saving models typically calibrate idiosyncratic income risk from unexplained income dispersion, yet these measures differ from household perceptions. Using data from the New York Fed’s Survey of Consumer Expectations, I directly calibrate perceived income risks and show they are lower and more heterogeneous than conventional estimates. This “perception wedge” arises from unobserved heterogeneity and overconfidence. Incorporating subjective beliefs into a heterogeneous-agent life-cycle model substantially improves the fit to observed wealth inequality and the prevalence of hand-to-mouth households. The results highlight the importance of the “choice channel,” whereby subjective risk perceptions shape wealth accumulation dynamics.},
